[[compiler-codegen]]
=== Code generation

[abstract]
Code generation is the last stage that works on the program's code. It takes
the per-language trees left by segmentation, serialization and ownership
planning, in which every call is resolved to one implementation in one
language and every crossing has a wire form, and turns each language's share
into the source text of one pool. This section specifies how pools are
assembled, how user types become native definitions, which values are fixed
at build time, and the properties every generated pool has whatever its
language. The manifest that describes the program's commands is built beside
the pools and is specified in <<compiler-interface>>.

**Input.** The unit of code generation is the *manifold*: a function the
compiler generates for a call site, together with the context it needs
(<<compiler-calls>>). Each manifold carries an integer id, unique within the
build, and a language. Manifolds are grouped by the pool they run in, which is
their language's own pool, except that a guest language's manifolds join the
pool of its host (<<compiler-onboarding>>). Within one pool every manifold,
nested ones included, is defined once under its id. Two different manifolds
that would share an id in one pool are an internal compiler error, since a
caller of that id would run whichever one was emitted.

**Build-time values.** Before translation, the intrinsics whose value is
fixed when the program is built are replaced by literals:

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Intrinsic | Replaced by
| `@version` | The compiler version that built the program.
| `@compiled` | The build time, as an ISO 8601 UTC timestamp.
| `@lang` | The name of the language whose pool evaluates it.
| `@datafile p` | The installed location of data file `p`, or `p` unchanged when the program is not installed.
|===

Every other intrinsic (`@save`, `@load`, `@hash`, `@show`, the stream
operations, ...) is a call into the language's runtime binding, which
implements it once for every program (<<intrinsics>>, <<compiler-libmorloc>>).

**Two translators.** Every language is translated in two steps: the
manifolds are lowered into one shared imperative form (statements,
expressions, function definitions, and two dispatch tables), and a
per-language printer writes that form as source text. The languages differ in
how much of this is data:

* A dynamically typed language (Python, R) is translated entirely from its
  language description: literals, list and record constructors, field and
  index access, function headers, lambdas, partial application, block style,
  and the names of the runtime calls that serialize, deserialize and call
  another pool are all templates in that description. No code specific to
  the language exists in the compiler.
* A statically typed language ({cpp}, Rust) has a dedicated translator,
  because it must render a native type for every value, emit type
  definitions in an order the compiler accepts, and generate marshalling code
  for each user type.

**Pool assembly.** Each language has a fixed pool template that holds the
runtime scaffolding: process start, the arguments a pool is started with, and
the hand-off to the runtime's pool loop. The generated code is spliced into
marked sections of that template:

[mermaid]
....
flowchart LR
    M[manifolds of one pool] --> L[lower to shared imperative form]
    L --> P[per-language printer]
    T[pool template] --> S
    P --> S[splice into sections]
    S --> O[pool source]
    O --> H[fingerprint]
....

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Section | Contents
| Sources | Imports or includes of the user's sourced files, and the language's preamble.
| Types | Native definitions of user types and their marshalling code (statically typed languages only).
| Declarations | Forward declarations of every manifold, so definition order never matters.
| Manifolds | One function per manifold, named by its id.
| Dispatch | A local table mapping each entry point's id to its function, and a remote table for calls that arrive as jobs from another node.
|===

The ids in the dispatch tables are the `mid` values the manifest records for
each command, and the ids a calling pool puts in its call packets
(<<build-packets>>).

**Inlining keeps evaluation order.** Not every application becomes a function
of its own; one that does not is written as an expression at its use. Such an
inlined application is evaluated exactly where the call it replaces would
have been: its statements go immediately before the statement that uses its
value, in the same block. A position that is not evaluated eagerly (an arm of
a conditional, the second operand of `&&` or `||`) is a block of its own, so
whatever is inlined there is evaluated only if that position is.

**Native types.** A morloc type reaches a language through its native form, a
template that a `type L => ...` declaration gives (<<mapping-native-types>>):
`type Cpp => (List a) = "std::vector<$1>" a` renders `[Int]` as
`std::vector<int>`, given `type Cpp => Int = "int"`. An alias with no form of its own
uses its expansion's. How user types appear in each language:

[cols="1,2,2", options="header"]
|===
| Language | Record | `data` type with payloads
| Python | An ordered `dict` keyed by field name. | Nothing is generated; the binding builds the value from its wire form.
| R | A named `list`. | Nothing is generated; the binding builds the value from its wire form.
| {cpp} | A struct the user defines and links with `record Cpp => R = "R"`. The compiler generates its marshalling code, which reads and writes each field by its morloc name. | Generated: a struct holding a `std::variant` of `std::shared_ptr<const Arm>`, one alternative per constructor in declaration order. A type with no payloads is a generated enum.
| Rust | A struct the user defines and links with `record Rust => R`. The compiler generates its marshalling code. | Generated, with its marshalling code.
|===

In {cpp} the declarations follow what holds what by value: enums first, then
variant wrappers (which hold their arms through pointers), then records,
then arm bodies, and every marshaller is declared before any is defined.

**Wrappers.** Three kinds of manifold get a wrapper around their body. A call
labeled for caching looks its key up before running and stores the result
after (<<eco-caching>>). A program built with `--debug` records every call in
its trace (<<build-errors>>). A tail-recursive function that the compiler can
run as a loop is emitted as one (<<compiler-calls>>).

**The fingerprint.** When every pool's source is final, the compiler hashes
each one and writes the hash into the manifest. The hash covers the pool's
emitted source text, the files the program lists under `hash-include`, the
build parameters, and any compiled guest objects linked into the pool, since
the last two change the binary without changing the source. It is the pool's
contribution to every cache key (<<eco-caching>>), so it is computed after
emission and before anything is written to disk.

**What every pool does.** Whatever its language, a generated pool:

* is started with three trailing arguments: its socket path, the run's
  temporary directory, and the shared-memory base name;
* is ready when it answers a ping on its socket (<<build-lifecycle>>);
* answers every call with exactly one packet, a value or a failure
  (<<build-packets>>);
* turns an error raised by user code into a failure of that call, and goes on
  serving;
* is entered only through its dispatch tables, by manifold id; calls within
  the pool are native calls.

A language may reserve name prefixes and names for what its pool defines. A
sourced name that uses one is rejected at build, so user code and generated
code cannot shadow each other.

How a pool spreads its calls over threads or processes is specified in
<<build-workers>>.

